Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Mosuo Sisters is a 2012 documentary film written and directed by Marlo Poras.","title":"The Mosuo Sisters"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It chronicles the lives of two sisters, Jua Ma and La Tsuo, who are members of one of the last matriarchal societies, the Mosuo tribe.","title":"The Mosuo Sisters"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Being an ethnic minority in China, the film explores their journey from working at a bar in Beijing to moving back home to their village in the Himalayas, dealing with the modern world impinging upon the traditional Mosuo culture and way of life.","title":"The Mosuo Sisters"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"4th Impact (formerly known as The Cercado Sisters, The Cercados, The Gollayan Sisters, MICA, and 4th Power) is a Filipino girl group composed of sisters Almira, Irene, Mylene, and Celina Cercado.","title":"4th Impact"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The group rose to popularity after competing in the 12th series of \"X Factor UK\", where they finished in fifth place.","title":"4th Impact"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" They are also known for having participated in several Philippine and international talent competitions, including the \"World Championships of Performing Arts\", where they won in 2006 and 2013, and the South Korean talent search \"Superstar K6\" in 2014, where they reached eighth place.","title":"4th Impact"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The sisters were originally from Santiago City, Isabela, but are now based in Metro Manila, Philippines.","title":"4th Impact"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Clark Sisters are an American gospel vocal group consisting of five sisters: Jacky Clark Chisholm (b. 1948), Denise Clark Bradford (b. 1953), Elbernita \"Twinkie\" Clark-Terrell (b. 1954), Dorinda Clark-Cole (b. 1957), and Karen Clark Sheard (b. 1960).","title":"The Clark Sisters"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Clark Sisters are the daughters of gospel musician and choral director Mattie Moss Clark.","title":"The Clark Sisters"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" They are credited for helping to bring gospel music to the mainstream and are considered as pioneers of contemporary gospel.","title":"The Clark Sisters"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Their biggest crossover hits include: \"Is My Living in Vain?\"","title":"The Clark Sisters"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":", \"Hallelujah\", \"He Gave Me Nothing to Lose\", \"Endow Me\", their hit song \"Jesus Is A Love Song\", \"Pure Gold\", \"Expect a Miracle\", and their largest mainstream crossover gold certified, \"You Brought the Sunshine\".","title":"The Clark Sisters"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" The Clark Sisters have won three Grammy Awards.","title":"The Clark Sisters"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" With 16 albums to their credit and millions in sales, the Clark Sisters are the highest-selling female gospel group in history.","title":"The Clark Sisters"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Walker Sisters Place was a homestead in the Great Smoky Mountains of Sevier County, in the U.S. state of Tennessee.","title":"Walker Sisters Place"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The surviving structures— which include the cabin, springhouse, and corn crib— were once part of a farm that belonged to the Walker Sisters— five spinster sisters who became local legends due to their adherence to traditional ways of living.","title":"Walker Sisters Place"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The sisters inherited the farm from their father, and after the Great Smoky Mountains National Park was formed in the 1930s, they obtained a lifetime lease.","title":"Walker Sisters Place"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The National Park Service gained control of the property in 1964 when the last Walker sister died.","title":"Walker Sisters Place"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" The surviving structures were placed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1976.","title":"Walker Sisters Place"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Sisters of Wellber (Japanese: ウエルベールの物語 〜Sisters of Wellber〜 , Hepburn: Werubēru no Monogatari ~Sisters of Wellber~ ) is a fantasy anime series animated by Trans Arts (with production assistance by Production I.G).","title":"Sisters of Wellber"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Directed by Takayuki Hamana (浜名 孝行 , Hamana Takayuki ) , the director behind the renowned Prince of Tennis TV series and animated movies, \"Sisters of Wellber\" began airing in Japan on Tokyo MX, Mētele, ABC, and AT-X in April 2007.","title":"Sisters of Wellber"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Ron Kelly (born June 11, 1929 in Vancouver, British Columbia) is a Canadian film director and screenwriter.","title":"Ron Kelly (filmmaker)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" He began his career with the CBC film unit, directing many short and documentary films between 1952 and 1964.","title":"Ron Kelly (filmmaker)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" He traveled to France, Spain and Mexico producing and directing documentaries independently from 1956 to 1958.","title":"Ron Kelly (filmmaker)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" From 1959 to 1962 he studied at Pinewood Studios, England, on a Canadian Arts Council fellowship and while there produced and directed documentaries for the BBC, CBC and National Office of Film, UK.","title":"Ron Kelly (filmmaker)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" In 1967 he co-wrote and directed the feature film Waiting for Caroline, as a commemoration of Canada's centennial year.","title":"Ron Kelly (filmmaker)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" \"Waiting for Caroline\" was distributed internationally by United Artists, Hollywood.","title":"Ron Kelly (filmmaker)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" In Hollywood in 1968, Kelly directed for Twentieth Century Fox Studios and Disney Studio.","title":"Ron Kelly (filmmaker)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" In 1970 he returned to Canada, writing, directing and producing dramas and documentaries for the NFB and CBC.","title":"Ron Kelly (filmmaker)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":8,"text":" His most recent film is \"Victims of Victims\" (2004), a documentary exploration of the Palestinian-Israeli conflict.","title":"Ron Kelly (filmmaker)"}

