Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Snow Flower and the Secret Fan is a 2005 novel by Lisa See set in nineteenth-century China.","title":"Snow Flower and the Secret Fan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" In her introduction to the novel, See writes that Lily, the narrator, was born on June 5, 1824 — \"the fifth day of the sixth month of the third year of the Daoguang Emperor's reign\".","title":"Snow Flower and the Secret Fan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The novel begins in 1903, when Lily is 80 years old.","title":"Snow Flower and the Secret Fan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" It continues on to tell the story of her life from birth, childhood, marriage, and old age.","title":"Snow Flower and the Secret Fan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" During her lifetime, Lily lives through the reigns of four emperors of the Qing dynasty: Daoguang (1820–1850); Xianfeng (1850–1861); Tongzhi (1861–1875); and Guangxu (1875–1908).","title":"Snow Flower and the Secret Fan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lý Thánh Tông (] ; born Lý Nhật Tôn ] , ruled 1054–1072) was the posthumous title of the third emperor of the Lý dynasty of Vietnam.","title":"Lý Thánh Tông"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Like his father, he was considered as one of the most talented and benevolent emperors in Vietnamese history.","title":"Lý Thánh Tông"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The history of the Roman Empire covers the history of Ancient Rome from the fall of the Roman Republic in 27 BC until the abdication of the last Western emperor in 476 AD.","title":"History of the Roman Empire"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Rome had begun expanding shortly after the founding of the Republic in the 6th century BC, though it did not expand outside of the Italian Peninsula until the 3rd century BC.","title":"History of the Roman Empire"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Civil war engulfed the Roman state in the mid 1st century BC, first between Julius Caesar and Pompey, and finally between Octavian and Mark Antony.","title":"History of the Roman Empire"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Antony was defeated at the Battle of Actium in 31 BC.","title":"History of the Roman Empire"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" In 27 BC the Senate and People of Rome made Octavian \"imperator\" (\"commander\") thus beginning the Principate, the first epoch of Roman imperial history usually dated from 27 BC to 284 AD; they later awarded him the name Augustus, \"the venerated\".","title":"History of the Roman Empire"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" The success of Augustus in establishing principles of dynastic succession was limited by his outliving a number of talented potential heirs: the Julio-Claudian dynasty lasted for four more emperors—Tiberius, Caligula, Claudius, and Nero—before it yielded in 69 AD to the strife-torn Year of Four Emperors, from which Vespasian emerged as victor.","title":"History of the Roman Empire"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" Vespasian became the founder of the brief Flavian dynasty, to be followed by the Nerva–Antonine dynasty which produced the \"Five Good Emperors\": Nerva, Trajan, Hadrian, Antoninus Pius and the philosophically inclined Marcus Aurelius.","title":"History of the Roman Empire"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" In the view of the Greek historian Dio Cassius, a contemporary observer, the accession of the emperor Commodus in 180 AD marked the descent \"from a kingdom of gold to one of rust and iron\"—a famous comment which has led some historians, notably Edward Gibbon, to take Commodus' reign as the beginning of the decline of the Roman Empire.","title":"History of the Roman Empire"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Army of the Mughal Empire was the force by which the Mughal emperors established their empire in the 15th century and expanded it to its greatest extent at the beginning of the 18th century.","title":"Army of the Mughal Empire"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Although its origins, like the Mughals themselves, were in the cavalry-based armies of central Asia, its essential form and structure was established by the empire's third emperor, Akbar.","title":"Army of the Mughal Empire"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Year of the Four Emperors was a year in the history of the Roman Empire, AD 69, in which four emperors ruled in succession: Galba, Otho, Vitellius, and Vespasian.","title":"Year of the Four Emperors"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Vitellius (Latin: \"Aulus Vitellius Germanicus Augustus\" ; 24 September AD 15 – 22 December AD 69) was Roman Emperor for eight months, from 16 April to 22 December AD 69.","title":"Vitellius"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Vitellius was proclaimed emperor following the quick succession of the previous emperors Galba and Otho, in a year of civil war known as the Year of the Four Emperors.","title":"Vitellius"}

