Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1999–2000 NBA season was the 53rd season of the National Basketball Association in New York City.","title":"1999–2000 New York Knicks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" During the offseason, the Knicks re-signed free agent John Wallace.","title":"1999–2000 New York Knicks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" In his second year with the Knicks, Latrell Sprewell became a starter after playing off the bench last season and averaged 18.6 points per game.","title":"1999–2000 New York Knicks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" After advancing to the NBA Finals as the #8 seed last year, the Knicks finished second in the Atlantic Division with a 50–32 record, good enough for their first 50-win season since 1997.","title":"1999–2000 New York Knicks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Allan Houston and head coach Jeff Van Gundy represented the Eastern Conference during the 2000 NBA All-Star Game.","title":"1999–2000 New York Knicks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" In the first round of the playoffs, the Knicks swept the Toronto Raptors in three straight games.","title":"1999–2000 New York Knicks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" In the semifinals, they faced the Miami Heat for the fourth consecutive year.","title":"1999–2000 New York Knicks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" They would defeat the 2nd-seeded Heat in a tough hard fought seven game series, but would lose in six games to the Indiana Pacers in the Eastern Conference Finals.","title":"1999–2000 New York Knicks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1967 NBA All-Star Game was the 17th All-Star Game played January 10, 1967, at the Cow Palace in San Francisco, California.","title":"1967 NBA All-Star Game"}
