Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Western Conference All-Stars defeated the Eastern Conference All-Stars, 135-120.","title":"1967 NBA All-Star Game"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The game featured the ejection of Red Auerbach, (who retired at the end of the previous season) head coach of the Eastern Conference, who became the only coach to be ejected in an All-Star Game.","title":"1967 NBA All-Star Game"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1999–2000 NBA season was the Bucks' 32nd season in the National Basketball Association.","title":"1999–2000 Milwaukee Bucks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" During the offseason, the Bucks acquired Danny Manning and former Bucks star Dale Ellis from the Orlando Magic, who acquired Manning from the Phoenix Suns, and Ellis from the Seattle SuperSonics, while signing free agent Darvin Ham.","title":"1999–2000 Milwaukee Bucks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" With Sam Cassell playing a full season after dealing with injuries, the Bucks played above .500 in the first half of the season.","title":"1999–2000 Milwaukee Bucks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" However, they struggled in February posting a 3–9 record as Ellis was traded to the Charlotte Hornets.","title":"1999–2000 Milwaukee Bucks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" With less than a month to go, the Bucks playoff chances appeared bleak as they had a 32–37 record in late March.","title":"1999–2000 Milwaukee Bucks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" However, down the stretch, they won 10 of their final 13 games to sneak into the playoffs as the #8 seed in the Eastern Conference, finishing fifth in the Central Division with a 42–40 record.","title":"1999–2000 Milwaukee Bucks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" Ray Allen and Glenn Robinson were both selected for the 2000 NBA All-Star Game.","title":"1999–2000 Milwaukee Bucks season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 2011–12 Miami Heat season is the franchise's 24th season in the National Basketball Association (NBA).","title":"2011–12 Miami Heat season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" They came into the season as the defending Eastern Conference champions, the second season playing with the \"Big Three\" of Dwyane Wade, LeBron James, and Chris Bosh, and the fourth season under head coach Erik Spoelstra.","title":"2011–12 Miami Heat season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Prior to the beginning of the season, they looked to bounce back from their disappointing finish to the previous year where they lost to the Dallas Mavericks in the NBA Finals.","title":"2011–12 Miami Heat season"}
