Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Patricia Valeria Bannister, (born November 21, 1923 in London, England; died November 18, 2009 in Bellevue, Washington, USA) was a writer of historical romance fiction from 1978 until 2002.","title":"Patricia Veryan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" She wrote under the names Patricia Veryan and Gwyneth Moore.","title":"Patricia Veryan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Morishima Chūryō (森島 中良 , 1756 – December 29, 1810) was an Edo period Japanese author of popular fiction who also wrote a number of works in the field of rangaku (Western studies).","title":"Morishima Chūryō"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" He wrote under many pen names, including Manzōtei, Shinra Manzō (or, conventionally, Shinra Banshō), and Tenjiku Rōjin (\"old man from India\").","title":"Morishima Chūryō"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The latter constituted an allusion to the pen name Tenjiku Rōnin (\"masterless samurai from India\"), used by Hiraga Gennai, to whom Chūryō was the principal literary successor.","title":"Morishima Chūryō"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Chūryō co-authored several plays with Gennai early in his career, and went on to write in almost all of the many genres of popular fiction that were collectively known as gesaku.","title":"Morishima Chūryō"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" He also wrote kyōka, or comic waka poetry, under the pen name Taketsue no Sugaru.","title":"Morishima Chūryō"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" Chūryō was the younger brother of Katsuragawa Hoshū, a shogunal physician and leading scholar of rangaku.","title":"Morishima Chūryō"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Ondine is a romance novel first published in 1988 under Heather Graham Pozzessere’s pen name, Shannon Drake.","title":"Ondine (novel)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is the story of a lady of 17th century England who finds herself cast from nobility when her father is accused of treason and murdered.","title":"Ondine (novel)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" As a noose rests at her neck, her one regret is that she did not have a chance to find his killer.","title":"Ondine (novel)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" She is once again given that chance when Lord Chatham, hoping to use the lady to solve the murder of his wife, takes her hand in marriage to save her from the hangman’s rope.","title":"Ondine (novel)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" The novel consists of multiple plot lines making it more than just an average romance novel.","title":"Ondine (novel)"}
