Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"\"The Loneliness of the Long-Distance Runner\" is a short story by Alan Sillitoe, published in 1959 as part of a short story collection of the same name.","title":"The Loneliness of the Long-Distance Runner"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The work focuses on Smith, a poor Nottingham teenager from a dismal home in a working class area, who has bleak prospects in life and few interests beyond petty crime.","title":"The Loneliness of the Long-Distance Runner"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The boy turns to long-distance running as a method of both an emotional and a physical escape from his situation.","title":"The Loneliness of the Long-Distance Runner"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The story was adapted for a 1962 film of the same title, with Sillitoe writing the screenplay and Tony Richardson directing.","title":"The Loneliness of the Long-Distance Runner"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" The part of Smith (now called Colin) was played by Tom Courtenay.","title":"The Loneliness of the Long-Distance Runner"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pablo de Rokha (October 17, 1895 in Licantén, Chile - December 10, 1968 in Santiago, Chile) was a Chilean poet.","title":"Pablo de Rokha"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" He won the Chilean Premio Nacional de Literatura (National Literature Prize) in 1965 and is counted among The four greats of Chilean poetry, along with Pablo Neruda, Vicente Huidobro and Gabriela Mistral.","title":"Pablo de Rokha"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" He is considered an avant-garde poet and an influential figure in the poetry scene of his country.","title":"Pablo de Rokha"}
