Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Rock and Roll Over Tour is a concert tour by the American hard rock group Kiss.","title":"Rock &amp; Roll Over Tour"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It began November 24, 1976 (shortly after the release of the Rock and Roll Over album) and ended April 4, 1977.","title":"Rock &amp; Roll Over Tour"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was on this tour where the band performed for the first time at Madison Square Garden on 2/18/77 - a venue all four members of the band had long dreamed of playing (February 18, 1977).","title":"Rock &amp; Roll Over Tour"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The band also performed in Japan for the first time on this tour.","title":"Rock &amp; Roll Over Tour"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" They played Budokan Hall four nights in a row breaking the previous concert attendance record set by The Beatles.","title":"Rock &amp; Roll Over Tour"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" On December 12, 1976 Ace Frehley suffered a severe electrical shock on stage which delayed the concert.","title":"Rock &amp; Roll Over Tour"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" The delay lasted about fifteen minutes and then resumed; Ace was able to finish the show despite having lost feeling in one of his hands.","title":"Rock &amp; Roll Over Tour"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" Being inspired by the event, Ace wrote the song \"Shock Me\" which appeared on Kiss' next album release Love Gun (1977).","title":"Rock &amp; Roll Over Tour"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":8,"text":" It is the first Kiss song in which Ace sings lead vocals.","title":"Rock &amp; Roll Over Tour"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":9,"text":" This was the first tour on which \"Beth\" was performed live; instead of being played by the entire band, it was sung solo by drummer Peter Criss to a recording of the instrumental track from the Destroyer album.","title":"Rock &amp; Roll Over Tour"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"\"Firm Biz\" is the first single by the Hip hop supergroup The Firm from their singular collaborative LP \"The Album\".","title":"Firm Biz"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The song was produced by L.E.S, who based the song's track on a sample of Teena Marie's 1981 hit \"Square Biz\".","title":"Firm Biz"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" \"Firm Biz\" also features a chorus performed by former En Vogue singer Dawn Robinson, reworked from the \"Square Biz\" chorus.","title":"Firm Biz"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" AZ raps the first verse of the song, Nas the second, and Foxy Brown the third and final verse.","title":"Firm Biz"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" The song never reached any US chart positions, but was a UK Top 20 hit in 1997.","title":"Firm Biz"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" A remix of the song was released, featuring a verse from Half-A-Mill and a chorus by Mary J. Blige.","title":"Firm Biz"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" The word \"feds\" is censored in the explicit version, in verse 2, performed by Nas, when he says \"Never that though Black .4-4's for feds\".","title":"Firm Biz"}
