Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"First and Last Things is a 1908 work of philosophy by H. G. Wells setting forth his beliefs in four \"books\" entitled \"Metaphysics,\" \"Of Belief,\" \"Of General Conduct,\" and \"Some Personal Things.\"","title":"First and Last Things"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Parts of the book were published in the \"Independent Magazine\" in July and August 1908.","title":"First and Last Things"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Wells revised the book extensively in 1917, in response to his religious conversion, but later published a further revision in 1929 that restored much of the book to its earlier form.","title":"First and Last Things"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Its main intellectual influences are Darwinism and certain German thinkers Wells had read, such as August Weismann.","title":"First and Last Things"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" The pragmatism of William James, who had become a friend of Wells, was also an influence.","title":"First and Last Things"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster is a 1924 biography of Frederick William Sanderson (1857-1922) by H. G. Wells.","title":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is the only biography Wells wrote.","title":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Sanderson was a personal friend, having met Wells in 1914 when his sons George Philip ('Gip'), born in 1901, and Frank Richard, born in 1903, became pupils at Oundle School, of which Sanderson was headmaster from 1892 to 1922.","title":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" After Sanderson died while giving a lecture at University College London at which he was introduced by Wells, the famous author agreed to help produce a biography to raise money for the school.","title":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" But in December 1922, after disagreements emerged with Sanderson's widow about his approach to the subject, Wells withdrew from the official biography (published in 1923 as \"Sanderson of Oundle\"; Wells wrote much of the text but the volume was published without listing an author) and published his own work separately.","title":"The Story of a Great Schoolmaster"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pierre Martory (December 1, 1920 – October 5, 1998) was a French poet whose influence on New York School poets was quiet but profound.","title":"Pierre Martory"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" His work was admired by Frank O'Hara, James Schuyler, Harry Mathews, and others, and translated extensively by John Ashbery, with whom he shared his life in Paris for nearly a decade.","title":"Pierre Martory"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" His work has appeared in many books in both England and the United States, as well as in \"The New Yorker\" and \"Poetry\".","title":"Pierre Martory"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Martory’s personal “charm,” the poet Ann Lauterbach once said, “devolved back to the original meaning of ‘spell.","title":"Pierre Martory"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":"’” However, as Ashbery has noted, “Both the humor and the sadness in his poems are always rendered with an unemphatic clarity that is certainly Mozartian.”","title":"Pierre Martory"}
