Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Imperial Japanese Army (IJA; 大日本帝國陸軍 \"Dai-Nippon Teikoku Rikugun\"; \"Army of the Greater Japanese Empire\") was the official ground-based armed force of the Empire of Japan from 1868 to 1945.","title":"Imperial Japanese Army"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was controlled by the Imperial Japanese Army General Staff Office and the Ministry of War, both of which were nominally subordinate to the Emperor of Japan as supreme commander of the army and the navy.","title":"Imperial Japanese Army"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Later an Inspectorate General of Aviation became the third agency with oversight of the army.","title":"Imperial Japanese Army"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" During wartime or national emergencies, the nominal command functions of the emperor would be centralized in an Imperial General Headquarters (IGHQ), an ad-hoc body consisting of the chief and vice chief of the Army General Staff, the Minister of War, the chief and vice chief of the Naval General Staff, the Inspector General of Aviation, and the Inspector General of Military Training.","title":"Imperial Japanese Army"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"In modern use, the order of battle of an armed force participating in a military operation or campaign shows the hierarchical organization, command structure, strength, disposition of personnel, and equipment of units and formations of the armed force.","title":"Order of battle"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Various abbreviations are in use, including OOB, O/B, or OB, while ORBAT remains the most common in the United Kingdom.","title":"Order of battle"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" An order of battle should be distinguished from a table of organisation, which is the intended composition of a given unit or formation according to the military doctrine of its armed force.","title":"Order of battle"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" As combat operations develop during a campaign, orders of battle may be revised and altered in response to the military needs and challenges.","title":"Order of battle"}
