Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Edith the Fair (Old English: \"Ealdgȳð Swann hnesce\" , \"Edyth the Gentle Swan\"; c. 1025 – c. 1086), also known as Edith Swanneck, was the first wife of King Harold Godwinson.","title":"Edith the Fair"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" \"Swanneck\" (or Swan-Neck) comes from the folk etymology which made her in Old English as \"swann hnecca\", \"swan neck\", which was actually most likely a corrupted form of \"swann hnesce, \"\"Gentle Swan\"\" \".","title":"Edith the Fair"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" She is sometimes confused with Ealdgyth, daughter of Earl Ælfgar of Mercia, who was queen during Harold's reign.","title":"Edith the Fair"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Last English King (1997) is a historical novel by English writer Julian Rathbone.","title":"The Last English King"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The novel covers the time of the Battle of Hastings.","title":"The Last English King"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" It revolves around Walt Edwinson, a housecarl of Harold Godwinson, the last Anglo-Saxon king of England.","title":"The Last English King"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The story starts with Walt returning to his home at Iwerne in Dorset four years after the Battle of Hastings.","title":"The Last English King"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" He had fled England after the defeat of the Anglo-Saxons and had spent the time travelling across Europe and Asia Minor.","title":"The Last English King"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" The story of his journey from Constantinople via Nicomedia and Nicaea to Side is then recounted in parallel with his recollections of the time before the battle, such as his accompanying Harold to William of Normandy's attack on Dinan.","title":"The Last English King"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Battle of Stamford Bridge took place at the village of Stamford Bridge, East Riding of Yorkshire, in England on 25 September 1066, between an English army under King Harold Godwinson and an invading Norwegian force led by King Harald Hardrada and the English king's brother Tostig Godwinson.","title":"Battle of Stamford Bridge"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" After a bloody battle, both Hardrada and Tostig along with most of the Norwegians were killed.","title":"Battle of Stamford Bridge"}
