Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Visayan spotted deer (\"Rusa alfredi\"), also known as the Philippine spotted deer, is a nocturnal and endangered species of deer located primarily in the rainforests of the Visayan islands of Panay and Negros though it once roamed other islands such as Cebu, Guimaras, Leyte, Masbate, and Samar.","title":"Visayan spotted deer"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is one of three endemic deer species in the Philippines, although it was not recognized as a separate species until 1983.","title":"Visayan spotted deer"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" An estimated 2,500 mature individuals survived worldwide as of 1996, according to the IUCN, although it is uncertain of how many of them still survive in the wild.","title":"Visayan spotted deer"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The diet of the deer, which consists of a variety of different types of grasses, leaves, and buds within the forest, is the primary indicator of its habitat.","title":"Visayan spotted deer"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Since 1991 the range of the species has severely decreased and is now almost co-extensive with that of the Visayan warty pig.","title":"Visayan spotted deer"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The red deer (\"Cervus elaphus\") is one of the largest deer species.","title":"Red deer"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The red deer inhabits most of Europe, the Caucasus Mountains region, Asia Minor, Iran, parts of western Asia, and central Asia.","title":"Red deer"}
