Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"\"Journaille \" is a German pejorative term used to refer to tabloid journalism and the yellow press.","title":"Journaille"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The term is a neologism from the early 20th century, formed from the word journalism and the French loan-word \"kanaille\" (\"canaille\"), meaning scum, scoundrel or rabble.","title":"Journaille"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The term was introduced by the Austrian writer Karl Kraus in an article in his journal \"Die Fackel\" in 1902.","title":"Journaille"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" In a later article in the same journal, Kraus wrote that the original inventor of the term was the Austrian dramaturge Alfred von Berger.","title":"Journaille"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" The pejorative term was much used by the German Nazi Party in their attacks on the press of the Weimar Republic.","title":"Journaille"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" Unlike many other terms used by the Nazis, the word \"journaille \" is still used in present-day Germany, and has also established itself in the political parlance of the Netherlands and Flanders.","title":"Journaille"}
