Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Saab 36 (also known as Projekt 1300) was a supersonic bomber planned by Saab during the 1950s.","title":"Saab 36"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The aircraft was intended to be able to carry an 800 kg free-falling nuclear weapon, but the Swedish nuclear weapons program was cancelled in the 1960s; the plans for the bomber had been cancelled in 1957.","title":"Saab 36"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Saab 36 was to be fitted with delta wings, as was the Saab 35 Draken fighter.","title":"Saab 36"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The engine was to be a version of the British Bristol Olympus turbojet, the same engine powering the Avro Vulcan jet bomber.","title":"Saab 36"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Avro Atlantic (Avro 722) was a proposed civilian airliner version of the British Avro Vulcan medium jet bomber.","title":"Avro Atlantic"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was a response to a 1952 UK Ministry of Supply requirement for a new aircraft suitable for both military and civilian long-range roles.","title":"Avro Atlantic"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Civilian models of the Vickers Valiant and Handley Page Victor V-bombers were also planned for the same contract.","title":"Avro Atlantic"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The Vickers V-1000 won the contest over the Atlantic, but ultimately none of these designs would be built.","title":"Avro Atlantic"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Avro Vulcan XH558 (military serial \"XH558\", civil aircraft registration \"G-VLCN\") \"The Spirit Of Great Britain\" was the last remaining airworthy example of the 134 Avro Vulcan jet powered delta winged strategic nuclear bomber aircraft operated by the Royal Air Force during the Cold War.","title":"Avro Vulcan XH558"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was the last Vulcan in military service, and the last to fly at all after 1986.","title":"Avro Vulcan XH558"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" It last flew on 28 October 2015.","title":"Avro Vulcan XH558"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Avro Vulcan (later Hawker Siddeley Vulcan from July 1963) is a jet-powered tailless delta wing high-altitude strategic bomber, which was operated by the Royal Air Force (RAF) from 1956 until 1984.","title":"Avro Vulcan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Aircraft manufacturer A.V. Roe and Company (Avro) designed the Vulcan in response to Specification B.35/46.","title":"Avro Vulcan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Of the three V bombers produced, the Vulcan was considered the most technically advanced and hence the riskiest option.","title":"Avro Vulcan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Several scale aircraft, designated Avro 707, were produced to test and refine the delta wing design principles.","title":"Avro Vulcan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Avro was a British aircraft manufacturer founded in 1910 whose designs include the Avro 504 used as a trainer in the First World War, the Avro Lancaster, one of the pre-eminent bombers of the Second World War, and the delta wing Avro Vulcan, a stalwart of the Cold War.","title":"Avro"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Roy Chadwick, CBE, FRSA, FRAeS (30 April 1893 – 23 August 1947) was an aircraft design engineer for the Avro Company.","title":"Roy Chadwick"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Born at Marsh Hall Farm, Farnworth, the son of the mechanical engineer Charles Chadwick, he was the chief designer for Avro and was responsible for practically all of their aeroplane designs.","title":"Roy Chadwick"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" He is famous in particular for designing the Avro Lancaster bomber, its follow-up Avro Lincoln and preliminary designs of the Avro Vulcan V bomber.","title":"Roy Chadwick"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" He also converted the Lincoln into the much-used Shackleton.","title":"Roy Chadwick"}

