Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Abū al-Husayn Bajkam al-Mākānī (Arabic: أبو الحسين بجكم المكاني‎ ‎ ), referred to as Bajkam, Badjkam or Bachkam (from \"Bäčkäm\", a Persian and Turkish word meaning a horse- or yak-tail), was a Turkish military commander and official of the Abbasid Caliphate.","title":"Bajkam"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" A former \"ghulam\" of the Ziyarid dynasty, Bajkam entered Abbasid service following the assassination of the Ziyarid ruler Mardavij in 935.","title":"Bajkam"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" During his five-year tenure at the Caliphate's court at Baghdad, he was granted the title of \"amir al-umara\", consolidating his dominance over the Caliphs ar-Radi and al-Muttaqi and giving him absolute power over their domains.","title":"Bajkam"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Bajkam was challenged throughout his rule by various opponents, including his predecessor as \"amir al-umara\", Muhammad ibn Ra'iq, the Basra-based Baridis, and the Buyid dynasty of Iran, but he succeeded in retaining control until his death.","title":"Bajkam"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" He was murdered by a party of Kurds during a hunting excursion in 941, shortly after the accession of al-Muttaqi as Caliph.","title":"Bajkam"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" Bajkam was known both for his firm rule and for his patronage of Baghdad intellectuals, who respected and in some cases befriended him.","title":"Bajkam"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" His death led to a void in central power, resulting in a brief period of instability and fighting in Baghdad.","title":"Bajkam"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Mugheri (or Mughery, Mughairi, Arabic: المغیری‎ ‎ ) are a social group descended from Arabs, however, in Pakistan they are considered to be Baloch.","title":"Mugheri"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" As per research, Mugheri are descended from an Arab Field Marshal called Mughera Bin Zaid Bin Hatim, who was appointed as a Field Marshal for Sindh by his elder brother and famous Governor of Sindh during Abbasid Caliphate named Daud Bin Zaid Bin Hatim.","title":"Mugheri"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Daud was appointed as a Governor of Sind in 800AD (184 Hijri) by Caliph Haroon-ur-Rashid (786-809).","title":"Mugheri"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Governor Daud Bin Zaid Bin Hatim governed Sind for the longest period of nine years and later his son Bashar Bin Daud Bin Hatim became the next successor Governor of Sind during the caliphate of Mamoon-ur-Rashid.","title":"Mugheri"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Additionally, the Mughera tribe was at the climax because they ran their business, trade, affairs and matters effectively and were considered as wealthy tribe under the Abbasid Caliphate.","title":"Mugheri"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Sa'id ibn Makhlad (died 889) was a senior official of the Abbasid Caliphate.","title":"Sa'id ibn Makhlad"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Born a Nestorian Christian, he converted to Islam and served as a secretary in the Abbasid capital, Baghdad.","title":"Sa'id ibn Makhlad"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" He rose to prominence during the regency of al-Muwaffaq over his brother, the Caliph al-Mu'tamid (r. 870–892): between 878 and 885, he served as \"de facto\" vizier of the Caliphate, although he did not bear the title.","title":"Sa'id ibn Makhlad"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" His valuable assistance to al-Muwaffaq was recognized in 882 by the award of the unique honorific title Dhu'l-Wizaratayn (\"possessor of the two vizierates\"), with which he appears even on coins.","title":"Sa'id ibn Makhlad"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" However, the activities of his brother, Abdun, who had remained a Christian and tried to obtain concessions for the Christian subjects of the Caliphate, brought about his sudden fall from power in 885.","title":"Sa'id ibn Makhlad"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" He died in 889.","title":"Sa'id ibn Makhlad"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Fourth Fitna or Great Abbasid Civil War resulted from the conflict between the brothers al-Amin and al-Ma'mun over the succession to the throne of the Abbasid Caliphate.","title":"Fourth Fitna"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Their father, Caliph Harun al-Rashid, had named al-Amin as the first successor, but had also named al-Ma'mun as the second, with Khurasan granted to him as an appanage.","title":"Fourth Fitna"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Later a third son, al-Qasim, had been designated as third successor.","title":"Fourth Fitna"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" After Harun died in 809, al-Amin succeeded him in Baghdad.","title":"Fourth Fitna"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Encouraged by the Baghdad court, al-Amin began trying to subvert the autonomous status of Khurasan, and al-Qasim was quickly sidelined.","title":"Fourth Fitna"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" In response, al-Ma'mun sought the support of the provincial élites of Khurasan and made moves to assert his own autonomy.","title":"Fourth Fitna"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" As the rift between the two brothers and their respective camps widened, al-Amin declared his own son Musa as his heir and assembled a large army.","title":"Fourth Fitna"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" In 811, al-Amin's troops marched against Khurasan, but al-Ma'mun's general Tahir ibn Husayn defeated them in the Battle of Rayy, and then invaded Iraq and besieged Baghdad itself.","title":"Fourth Fitna"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":8,"text":" The city fell after a year, al-Amin was executed, and al-Ma'mun became Caliph.","title":"Fourth Fitna"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Nahrawan Canal (Arabic: قناة النهروان‎ ‎ ) was a major irrigation system of the Sassanid and early Islamic periods in central Iraq, along the eastern banks of the Tigris and the lower course of the Diyala River.","title":"Nahrawan Canal"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Created in the 6th century, it reached its peak under the Abbasid Caliphate, when it served the main water supply for the Abbasid capital of Baghdad, while the regions irrigated by it served as the city's main breadbasket.","title":"Nahrawan Canal"}
