Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Ascension night heron (\"Nycticorax olsoni\") is an extinct night heron species from the genus \"Nycticorax\" endemic to the South Atlantic island of Ascension.","title":"Ascension night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is predominantly known from the bone fragments of six specimens found in guano deposits and caves on Ascension Island and described by Philip Ashmole, Kenneth Edwin Laurence Ryder Simmons, and William Richmond Postle Bourne in 2003.","title":"Ascension night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Lydenburg Heads refer to seven terracotta heads that were discovered in association with other pottery artefacts in Lydenburg, Mpumalanga, South Africa.","title":"Lydenburg heads"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" They are among the oldest known African Iron Age artworks from below the equator.","title":"Lydenburg heads"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Other artefacts found in association with these heads include ceramic vessels, iron and copper beads, and bone fragments.","title":"Lydenburg heads"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Charcoal associated with the heads was Radiocarbon dated, and this relative dating technique places these artefacts and the site at around 1410 BP (approximately 500 A.D.), which constitutes one of the earliest dates for an Iron Age settlement in South Africa.","title":"Lydenburg heads"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Mauritius night heron (\"Nycticorax mauritianus\") is an extinct night heron species from Mauritius.","title":"Mauritius night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is only known by seven subfossil bone remains consisted of cranium, pelvis, coracoid, ulna, radius, and tarsometatarsus found in Mare aux Songes.","title":"Mauritius night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Only the coracoid and the tarsometatarsus are left today.","title":"Mauritius night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" It was scientifically discussed in 1893 by Alfred Newton and Hans Gadow from the Cambridge University.","title":"Mauritius night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Newton and Gadow measured the tarsometatarsus with 81 to 87 mm.","title":"Mauritius night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" It became presumedly extinct in the late 17th century and was probably first mentioned by François Leguat in 1693 who described them as \"great flight of bitterns\".","title":"Mauritius night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Bermuda night heron (\"Nyctanassa carcinocatactes\") is an extinct heron species from Bermuda.","title":"Bermuda night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is sometimes assigned to the genus \"Nycticorax\".","title":"Bermuda night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was first described in 2006 by Storrs L. Olson and David B. Wingate from subfossil material found in the Pleistocene and Holocene deposits in caves and ponds of Bermuda.","title":"Bermuda night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Its anatomy was rather similar to its living relative, the yellow-crowned night heron (\"N. violacea\"), but it had a heavier bill, a more massive skull and more robust hind limbs.","title":"Bermuda night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" The specialization of the bill and the hind limbs showed that it was apparently adapted to the feeding on land crabs.","title":"Bermuda night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" There are also early historian reports referring to that species.","title":"Bermuda night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" It possibly became extinct due to the settlement of the Bermuda islands in the 17th century.","title":"Bermuda night heron"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The expression \"Giant of Castelnau\" refers to three bone fragments (a humerus, tibia, and femoral mid-shaft) discovered by Georges Vacher de Lapouge in 1890 in the sediment used to cover a Bronze Age burial tumulus, and then possibly dating back to the Neolithic.","title":"Giant of Castelnau"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" According to de Lapouge, the fossil bones may belong to one of the largest humans known to have existed.","title":"Giant of Castelnau"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" He estimated from the bone size that the human may have been about 3.50 metres tall.","title":"Giant of Castelnau"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" No modern peer-reviewed study has been published about the alleged giant bone fragments.","title":"Giant of Castelnau"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Bone Cabin Quarry was a dinosaur quarry that laid approximately fifty-five miles northwest of Laramie, Wyoming near historic Como Bluff.","title":"Bone Cabin Quarry"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" During the summer of 1897 Walter W. Granger, a paleontologist from the American Museum of Natural History, came upon a hillside littered with Jurassic period dinosaur bone fragments.","title":"Bone Cabin Quarry"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Nearby was a sheepherder's cabin built entirely out of fossil bones, hence the name \"Bone Cabin Quarry.\"","title":"Bone Cabin Quarry"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" After Granger's discovery in late August 1897, the quarry was kept secret until the summer of 1898, when the manpower could be amassed to undertake a full-scale excavation.","title":"Bone Cabin Quarry"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Bone Cabin Quarry was excavated from 1898 until 1905, when the productivity of specimens thinned.","title":"Bone Cabin Quarry"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" Some of the dinosaurs found at the Bone Cabin Quarry include \"Stegosaurus\", \"Allosaurus\" and \"Apatosaurus\". \"","title":"Bone Cabin Quarry"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":"Gargoyleosaurus\" is also known from the Bone Cabin Quarry West locality.","title":"Bone Cabin Quarry"}

