Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The marble Kritios Boy or Kritian Boy belongs to the Early Classical period of ancient Greek sculpture.","title":"Kritios Boy"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is the first statue from classical antiquity known to use contrapposto; Kenneth Clark called it \"the first beautiful nude in art\" It is possible, even likely, that earlier Bronze statues had used the technique, but if they did, they have not survived and Susan Woodford has speculated that the statue is a copy of a Bronze original.","title":"Kritios Boy"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Kritios Boy is thus named because it is attributed, on slender evidence, to Kritios who worked together with Nesiotes (sculptors of \"Harmodius and Aristogeiton\") or their school, from around 480 BC.","title":"Kritios Boy"}
