Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Indian Actors Association was formed around 1936 and was a non-profit Hollywood based organization.","title":"Indian Actors Association"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" They formed after and were influenced by the War Paint Club, an organization meant to protect rights of Native American actors.","title":"Indian Actors Association"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Additionally, driven by the Hollywood practice of casting non-native actors as Indians in films, the Native actors working in Los Angeles at the time seized their opportunity to establish a pool of “authentic” Indian actors to work in film.","title":"Indian Actors Association"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Their goal was also to keep Indian portrayals and culture from being misrepresented or warped.","title":"Indian Actors Association"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" The Indian Actors Association fought for equality and employment for Indian actors at a time when they were not seen as important or equal.","title":"Indian Actors Association"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" The country’s economic depression and the Western movie hiatus left many Indian actors unemployed, which caused depression and poverty on the reservations resulting in many Indians looking for employment elsewhere.","title":"Indian Actors Association"}

