Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Police perjury (or testilying in United States police slang) is the act of a police officer giving false testimony.","title":"Police perjury"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is typically used in a criminal trial to \"make the case\" against a defendant who the police believe to be guilty when irregularities during the suspect's arrest or search threaten to result in acquittal.","title":"Police perjury"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" It has broader meanings.","title":"Police perjury"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" It also can be extended further to encompass substantive misstatements of fact for the purpose of convicting those whom the police believe to be guilty, or even to include statements to frame an innocent citizen.","title":"Police perjury"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" More generically, it has been said to be: \"Lying under oath, especially by a police officer, to help get a conviction.\"","title":"Police perjury"}

