Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Basin and range topography is an alternating landscape of parallel mountain ranges and valleys.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is a result of crustal extension/stretching (extensional tectonics) of the lithosphere (crust and upper mantle) due to mantle upwelling, gravitational collapse, crustal thickening, or relaxation of confining stresses.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Crustal extension causes the thinning and deformation of the upper crust in an orientation perpendicular to the direction of extension.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" As the plates pull apart, they thin allowing the hot mantle to rise close to the surface.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" When the crust is extended it fractures along a fault plane, creating a series of long parallel normal faults. Between these normal faults are blocks, which subside, get uplifted or tilted.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" This is known as block faulting.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" Basins are formed due to subsidence of a block, while the blocks adjacent to the subsidence gets uplifted creating ranges.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" Normal faults are on both sides the blocks; creating alternating elevated or subsided blocks, otherwise known as horst and graben.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":8,"text":" Basins and ranges can also be formed by blocks that are tilted causing one side to subside while the other side gets uplifted.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":9,"text":" These only have one side with a normal fault, this is known as tilted block faulting.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":10,"text":" Extension causes the plate to stretch, fracture and thin.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":11,"text":" Mountains rise and valleys drop, over a long period of time creating what we see as basin and range topography.","title":"Basin and range topography"}
