Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Interbay is a neighborhood in Seattle, Washington in the United States consisting of the valley between Queen Anne Hill on the east and Magnolia on the west, plus filled-in areas of Smith Cove and Salmon Bay.","title":"Interbay, Seattle"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The neighborhood is bounded on the north by Salmon Bay, part of the Lake Washington Ship Canal, across which is Ballard; on the south by what remains of Smith Cove, an inlet of Elliott Bay; on the east by 15th Avenue W. and Elliott Avenue W.; and on the west by Thorndyke, 20th, and Gilman Avenues W.","title":"Interbay, Seattle"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Ballard Bridge crosses the ship canal from Interbay to Ballard.","title":"Interbay, Seattle"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Conibear Shellhouse is a rowing training and support facility in Seattle, Washington, on the campus of the University of Washington.","title":"Conibear Shellhouse"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is used by the men's and women's rowing teams of the Washington Huskies.","title":"Conibear Shellhouse"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The building was completed in 1949 and renovated in 2005.","title":"Conibear Shellhouse"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" It is located on Lake Washington, near the Lake Washington Ship Canal.","title":"Conibear Shellhouse"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lake Union is a freshwater lake entirely within the Seattle, Washington city limits and a major portion of the Lake Washington Ship Canal.","title":"Lake Union"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Its easternmost point is the Ship Canal Bridge, which carries Interstate 5 over the eastern arm of the lake and separates Lake Union from Portage Bay.","title":"Lake Union"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Lake Union is the namesake of the neighborhoods located on its east and west shores: Eastlake and Westlake, respectively.","title":"Lake Union"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The northern shore of the lake is home to Gas Works Park.","title":"Lake Union"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Notable features of the southern portion of the lake—collectively known as the South Lake Union district—include Lake Union Park, Museum of History & Industry (MOHAI), and the Center for Wooden Boats.","title":"Lake Union"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Ship Canal Bridge is a double-deck steel truss bridge that carries Interstate 5 (I-5) over Seattle's Portage Bay (part of the Lake Washington Ship Canal, after which it is named) between Capitol Hill and the University District.","title":"Ship Canal Bridge"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The canal below connects Lake Union with Lake Washington.","title":"Ship Canal Bridge"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Construction was completed in 1961 and the bridge opened to traffic on December 18, 1962.","title":"Ship Canal Bridge"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" It is 4,429 ft (1,350 meters) long, stands 182 feet above the canal and is 119 feet wide at the upper deck.","title":"Ship Canal Bridge"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" It was the largest bridge of its kind in the Northwest when it first opened.","title":"Ship Canal Bridge"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" The bridge is double-decked, with the upper deck carrying traffic in both directions and the lower deck (the express lanes) carrying traffic southbound in the morning and northbound in the afternoon.","title":"Ship Canal Bridge"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Lake Washington Ship Canal, which runs through the city of Seattle, connects the fresh water body of Lake Washington with the salt water inland sea of Puget Sound.","title":"Lake Washington Ship Canal"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Hiram M. Chittenden Locks accommodate the approximately 20 ft difference in water level between Lake Washington and the sound.","title":"Lake Washington Ship Canal"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The canal runs east–west and connects Union Bay, the Montlake Cut, Portage Bay, Lake Union, the Fremont Cut, Salmon Bay, and Shilshole Bay, which is part of the sound.","title":"Lake Washington Ship Canal"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Fremont Cut is a body of water that forms part of the Lake Washington Ship Canal, which passes through the U.S. city of Seattle and links Lake Washington to Puget Sound.","title":"Fremont Cut"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Fremont Cut connects Lake Union to the east with Salmon Bay to the west.","title":"Fremont Cut"}
