Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Wish You Were Here is the ninth studio album by English rock band Pink Floyd.","title":"Wish You Were Here (Pink Floyd album)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was released on 12 September 1975 by Harvest Records in the United Kingdom and a day later by Columbia Records in the United States.","title":"Wish You Were Here (Pink Floyd album)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Inspired by material the group composed while performing around Europe, \"Wish You Were Here\" was recorded during numerous recording sessions at Abbey Road Studios in London, England.","title":"Wish You Were Here (Pink Floyd album)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Two of the album's four songs criticise the music business, another expresses alienation and the multi-part track \"Shine On You Crazy Diamond\" is a tribute to Syd Barrett.","title":"Wish You Were Here (Pink Floyd album)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Barrett's mental breakdown had forced him to leave the group seven years earlier, prior to the release of the group's second studio album \"A Saucerful of Secrets\" (on which he only appeared on three tracks).","title":"Wish You Were Here (Pink Floyd album)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" It was lead writer Roger Waters' idea to split \"Shine On You Crazy Diamond\" into two parts that would bookend the album around three new compositions and to introduce a concept linking them all.","title":"Wish You Were Here (Pink Floyd album)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" The band had used a linking concept for their previous album, \"The Dark Side of the Moon\", to great success.","title":"Wish You Were Here (Pink Floyd album)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" As with \"The Dark Side of the Moon\", the band used studio effects and synthesizers and brought in guest singers to supply vocals on some tracks of the album.","title":"Wish You Were Here (Pink Floyd album)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":8,"text":" These singers were Roy Harper, who provided the lead vocals on \"Have a Cigar\", and the Blackberries, who added backing vocals to \"Shine On You Crazy Diamond\".","title":"Wish You Were Here (Pink Floyd album)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"\"Baby Lemonade\" is the opening track to Syd Barrett's second studio album, \"Barrett\".","title":"Baby Lemonade"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" \"Baby Lemonade\", and another song, \"Gigolo Aunt\", they were a go for Barrett to play and/or sing to an existing backing track.","title":"Baby Lemonade"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The solo was performed by Barrett, not David Gilmour as is often noted.","title":"Baby Lemonade"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The intro was actually Barrett simply warming-up on guitar, that Gilmour had managed to record and placed it at the start of the album, making it seem like an intro to the song.","title":"Baby Lemonade"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" It was included on the multi-artist Harvest compilation, \"A Breath of Fresh Air – A Harvest Records Anthology 1969–1974\" in 2007.","title":"Baby Lemonade"}
