Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Beaux-Arts architecture ( ; ] ) expresses the academic neoclassical architectural style taught at the \"École des Beaux-Arts\" in Paris.","title":"Beaux-Arts architecture"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The \"style\" \"Beaux Arts\" is the cumulative product of two-and-a-half centuries of instruction under the authority, first, of the \"Académie royale d'architecture\" (1671–1793), then, following the French Revolution of the late 18th century, of the Architecture section of the \"Académie des Beaux-Arts\" (1795– ).","title":"Beaux-Arts architecture"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The organization under the \"Ancien Régime\" of the competition for the \"Grand Prix de Rome\" in architecture, offering a chance to study in Rome, imprinted its codes and aesthetic on the course of instruction, which culminated during the Second Empire (1852–1870) and the Third Republic that followed.","title":"Beaux-Arts architecture"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The style of instruction that produced Beaux-Arts architecture continued without major interruption until 1968.","title":"Beaux-Arts architecture"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Detlef Lienau (February 17, 1818 Uetersen – August 29, 1887) was a German architect born in Holstein.","title":"Detlef Lienau"}
