Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The team lost all sixteen of their games in 2008, becoming the first team since the 1976 Tampa Bay Buccaneers to end an NFL season with no wins and no tied games as well as the first and only team to do it since the schedule was expanded to sixteen games.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" The Lions were mathematically eliminated from the playoffs by Week 11, when they stood 0–10.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" The team's victory over the Kansas City Chiefs in Week 16 of 2007 stood as their last until Week 3 of the 2009 season.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" From the time the Lions recorded a win over the Denver Broncos in 2007 to reach 6–2, the team went 5–47 over their following 52 games (including all of 2008 and 2009) before winning four games in a row at the end of the 2010 season.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" As of 2017, this is the only team in NFL history to finish a season with a 0–16 record.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":8,"text":" The 2016 Cleveland Browns and the 2009 St. Louis Rams came close to 0–16, both going 1–15.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"}
