Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Executive Council of Alberta, or more commonly the Cabinet of Alberta, is the Province of Alberta's equivalent to the Cabinet of Canada.","title":"Executive Council of Alberta"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The government of the province of Alberta is a constitutional monarchy and parliamentary democracy with a unicameral legislature—the Legislative Assembly, which consists of 87 members elected first past the post (FPTP) from single-member constituencies.","title":"Executive Council of Alberta"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The premier is normally a member of the Legislative Assembly, and usually draws the members of Cabinet from among the members of the Legislative Assembly.","title":"Executive Council of Alberta"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The legislative powers in the province however, lie with the Legislative Assembly of Alberta.","title":"Executive Council of Alberta"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Its government resembles that of the other Canadian provinces.","title":"Executive Council of Alberta"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" The capital of the province is Edmonton, where the Alberta Legislative Building is located.","title":"Executive Council of Alberta"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" Government is conducted after the Westminster model.","title":"Executive Council of Alberta"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch), also known as NSW Labor, is the New South Wales branch of the Australian Labor Party.","title":"Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The parliamentary leader is elected from and by the members of the party caucus, comprising all party members in the Legislative Assembly and Legislative Council.","title":"Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" The party factions have a strong influence on the election of the leader.","title":"Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" The leader's position is dependent on the continuing support of the caucus (and party factions) and the leader may be deposed by failing to win a vote of confidence of parliamentary members.","title":"Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" By convention, the premier sits in the Legislative Assembly, and is the leader of the party controlling a majority in that house.","title":"Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" The party leader also typically is a member of the Assembly, though this is not a strict party constitutional requirement.","title":"Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" Barrie Unsworth, for example, was elected party leader while a member of the Legislative Council.","title":"Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" He then transferred to the Assembly by winning a seat at a by-election.","title":"Australian Labor Party (New South Wales Branch)"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"S. T. Adityan (27 May 1904 – 1981) was an Indian politician from Tamil Nadu belonging to Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam party.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" A native of Kayamozhi, he was born in Perumalpuram in Tirunelveli district.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" He studied in England and was a lawyer by profession.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" He married Govindammal in 1933, who was the daughter of a very wealthy businessmen in Singapore, and with that wealth he managed to establish the Sun Paper Mills after returning to India.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" He began his life as a newspaper artist.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" He was imprisoned for four months in 1941 during Independence movement.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" He also participated actively in temple entry movement.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" He served as a director of Sun Paper Mill Ltd from 1961.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":8,"text":" He was lawyer by profession and served as an Advocate in Supreme Court of India.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":9,"text":" He served as the member of Central Legislative assembly between 1945-1947.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":10,"text":" He also served as a member of Tamil Nadu legislative assembly between 1952-1957.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":11,"text":" He was elected to the Tamil Nadu legislative assembly as an Indian National Congress candidate from Tiruchendur constituency in 1952 election.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":12,"text":" He was one of the two elected members from that constituency, the other being V. Arumugam (Indian politician) from Kisan Mazdoor Praja Party.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":13,"text":" He also served as a member of Tamil Nadu legislative council between 1958–1964 and 1967-1971.","title":"S. T. Adityan"}

