Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The roots of classical music in Kosovo are found in the 1940s and include the time period from the times when Kosovo was part of Yugoslavia to this day.","title":"Classical music in Kosovo"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" It can be said that there is a tradition of classical music in Kosovo, however, compared to other Balkan countries and especially European countries this tradition is younger.","title":"Classical music in Kosovo"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Classical music in Kosovo reaches back about 70 years.","title":"Classical music in Kosovo"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Even though in a short period of time, this music has evolved, passing through generations of composers and artists.","title":"Classical music in Kosovo"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" In his book \"Zhvillimi i stileve në veprat e kompozitorëve shqiptarë të Kosovës\", Engjëll Berisha comments:","title":"Classical music in Kosovo"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Musical composition can refer to an original piece of music, either a song or an instrumental music piece, the structure of a musical piece, or the process of creating or writing a new song or piece of music.","title":"Musical composition"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" People who create new compositions are called composers in classical music.","title":"Musical composition"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" In popular music and traditional music, the creators of new songs are usually called songwriters; with songs, the person who writes new words for a song is the lyricist.","title":"Musical composition"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" \"Composition\" is the act or practice of creating a song or other piece of music.","title":"Musical composition"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" In many cultures, including Western classical music, the act of composing typically includes the creation of music notation, such as a sheet music \"score\", which is then performed by the composer or by other instrumental musicians or singers.","title":"Musical composition"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" In popular music and traditional music, songwriting may involve the creation of a basic outline of the song, called the lead sheet, which sets out the melody, lyrics and chord progression.","title":"Musical composition"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" In classical music, orchestration (choosing the instruments of a large music ensemble such as an orchestra which will play the different parts of music, such as the melody, accompaniment, countermelody, bassline and so on) is typically done by the composer, but in musical theatre and in pop music, songwriters may hire an arranger to do the orchestration.","title":"Musical composition"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" In some cases, a pop or traditional songwriter may not use written notation at all, and instead compose the song in her mind and then play, sing and/or record it from memory.","title":"Musical composition"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":8,"text":" In jazz and popular music, notable sound recordings by influential performers are given the weight that written or printed scores play in classical music.","title":"Musical composition"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Odissi music (\"oṛiśī\") is a genre of classical music in India originated from the eastern state of Odisha.","title":"Odissi music"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" Indian classical music has five significant branches: Avanti, Panchali, Odramagadhi, Hindustani and Carnatic.","title":"Odissi music"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" Of these, Odramagadhi exists in the form of Odissi music.","title":"Odissi music"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Generally, Odissi is one of the classical dances of India performed with Odissi music.","title":"Odissi music"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":4,"text":" Odissi music was shaped during the time of Oriya poet Jayadeva, who composed lyrics meant to be sung.","title":"Odissi music"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":5,"text":" By the 11th century AD, folk music of Odisha in the form of Triswari, Chatuhswari, and Panchaswari was modified into the classical style.","title":"Odissi music"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":6,"text":" However, Odissi songs were written even before the Oriya language developed.","title":"Odissi music"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":7,"text":" Odissi music has a rich legacy dating back to the 2nd century BCE, when king Kharvela, the ruler of Odisha (Kalinga), patronized this music and dance.","title":"Odissi music"}

